        ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x} + x + 1$ at $\\left(1,3\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x} + x + 1$. Rewrite with powers, then differentiate term by term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=(1x^{\\frac{1}{3}})+(1x^{1})+(1)\\\\\nf'(x)&=1 + \\frac{1}{3 x^{\\frac{2}{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(1\\right)=1 + \\frac{1}{3 \\left(1\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}}}=\\frac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}=- \\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(3\\right)&=- \\frac{3}{4}\\left(x-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=- \\frac{3 x}{4}+\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)+\\left(3\\right)\\\\\ny&=\\frac{15}{4} - \\frac{3 x}{4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=- \\frac{3}{4}$ and $b=\\frac{15}{4}$."
