        ":question/id": "0fef9ef4-06cc-5ee7-9b0d-4fa0aee2b195",
        ":question/problem": "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2 \\sqrt[3]{x} - x + 1$ at $\\left(1,2\\right)$.\n\nGive exact values of $a$ and $b$ in $y=ax+b$.\n\n$a=$ {{slope}}\n\n$b=$ {{intercept}}",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=2 \\sqrt[3]{x} - x + 1$. Rewrite with powers, then differentiate term by term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=(2x^{\\frac{1}{3}})+(-1x^{1})+(1)\\\\\nf'(x)&=-1 + \\frac{2}{3 x^{\\frac{2}{3}}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the tangent slope at the supplied abscissa:\n\n$$\nm_t=f'\\left(1\\right)=-1 + \\frac{2}{3 \\left(1\\right)^{\\frac{2}{3}}}=- \\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so\n\n$$\nm_n=-\\frac{1}{m_t}=-\\frac{1}{\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)}=3.\n$$\n\nUse the point in point-slope form and rearrange:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny-\\left(2\\right)&=3\\left(x-\\left(1\\right)\\right)\\\\\ny&=3 x+\\left(-3\\right)+\\left(2\\right)\\\\\ny&=3 x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus $a=3$ and $b=-1$."
